- op: discuss  # bb13
  at: '2026-05-17T04:42:42Z'  # bb13
  by: agent  # bb13
  actor: jgstern_agent  # bb13
  clock: 3  # bb13
  nonce: bb13  # bb13
  message: "SCOPE EXPANSION (2026-05-17 verification): the gap is NOT Python-specific. Verified that add_failed_file() has ZERO production callers anywhere in the codebase. Three drop-site patterns confirmed:\n\n1. **Tree-sitter analyzers (universal — affects C, C++, Java, JS/TS, Go, Rust, Ruby, Kotlin, C#, Swift, ~15+ others):** analyze/base.py:2096-2100 catches OSError on read_bytes(), bumps files_skipped, continues. Single base-class location — one fix covers all TreeSitterAnalyzer subclasses.\n\n2. **Python analyzer:** py.py:2088 catches (SyntaxError, UnicodeDecodeError), returns None; caller at py.py:3404 bumps files_skipped. Distinct from #1 because ast.parse raises on syntax errors (tree-sitter doesn't — it emits ERROR nodes).\n\n3. **Linkers:** ipc.py:467, go_cobra.py:185, phoenix_ipc.py:248, xml_config.py:586, r_lang.py:483, csharp.py:1555+ etc. all bump files_skipped on OSError without recording.\n\n23 production files increment files_skipped. cli.py:7574-7575 then sets a generic 'some files skipped during analysis' on limits.partial_results_reason — losing all per-file granularity.\n\nEmpirical confirmation: a BOM-prefixed .py file (Python-path drop) and a permission-denied .go file (tree-sitter-path drop) both produce: failed_files=[], partial_results_reason='some files skipped during analysis'.\n\nRecommended fix scope:\n- Python analyzer (py.py:3398-3404): plumb path+reason to limits, not just a count\n- TreeSitterAnalyzer base (base.py:2096-2100): same — pass Limits in, record there\n- Linkers: same pattern\n- Single-source helper in limits.py would be cleanest."  # bb13
